WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:02.460 align:start position:0% line:0%
A living building
is really a building

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:06.131 align:start position:0% line:0%
that is a positive contributor
to its broader ecology

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:08.508 align:start position:0% line:0%
throughout every stage
of its life.

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:11.386
So conventional buildings
are responsible for

00:00:11.386 --> 00:00:15.014
about 37% of global
greenhouse gas emissions.

00:00:15.223 --> 00:00:19.185
And given the fact that we spend
about 90% of our time in them,

00:00:19.436 --> 00:00:20.770
they tend to off-gas

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.773
a ton of harmful chemicals
into our indoor air

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:27.485
and also contribute to a growing
pollution and waste problem.

00:00:27.944 --> 00:00:31.364
A living building
takes responsibility in account

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.451
for everything
that comes into its DNA

00:00:34.951 --> 00:00:37.829
and all of the effluents that
flow out into its environment.

00:00:37.954 --> 00:00:38.955
It's really exciting

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:42.250
to have the living village here
on campus at Yale

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:44.753
because it opens up
an opportunity

00:00:44.753 --> 00:00:46.713
for us to get to understand
and learn

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:49.716
about our local climate,
our flora, our fauna,

00:00:50.258 --> 00:00:54.012
and in doing so, engage
and prototype new ways

00:00:54.012 --> 00:00:55.221
of living together.

